# LeakSanitizer suppressions — THIRD-PARTY CODE ONLY.
#
# Same rule as tsan.supp: every entry is scoped to a shared object Lumen does not build.
# Patterns are substring matches against the symbolized frame, so no entry may name a
# function — only a library. A Lumen leak must never be able to match one of these.
#
# ── Mesa EGL / GL stack ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Bringing up an EGL display allocates driver-global state that Mesa never frees, and
# creating a GL context allocates gallium state with the same lifetime. Both are reachable
# for the life of the process and released only by process exit, which is exactly what LSan
# reports as a leak at exit.
#
# These resolve to a real shared object, which is why they get a proper suppression rather
# than the blunt `detect_leaks=0` the Vulkan tests need — the Vulkan ICD is dlclose'd
# before the leak check runs, so its frames degrade to "<unknown module>" and no pattern
# can match them. See TEST.md.
#
# libgallium carries its Mesa version in the filename (libgallium-26.1.6-arch1.1.so), so
# the pattern is deliberately a prefix.
leak:libEGL_mesa.so
leak:libgallium
leak:libgbm.so
leak:libglapi.so
